The tokens used by memcomp can outlive individual reclaim attempts and
name resident, compressed, same-filled or swap-backed contents. Exercise
their bounds, reference ownership and state transitions without relying
solely on a userspace workload observing a compressed PTE.

Add 425 named cases covering slot layouts, per-key storage, allocation
cancellation, lookup guards, resident records, rollback and fault
restoration. Check single-page and multi-page geometry, migration, page-
cache replacement, source-object-cgroup accounting, writeback and
swapoff lifetime rules.

Cover zero PTE and cache ownership separately from slot references,
including successful publication and losing transactions. Exercise
bounded metadata aging and writeback scans, asynchronous completion and
finish failures, same-filled values and incompressible content.

Retain assertions that only genuine -E2BIG results set the
incompressible marker, that rollback installs the folio hash and that
unchanged contents skip recompression. Reject KSM folios with a direct
eligibility check. Guard advice-dependent cases so configurations
without those syscalls still link and report their configuration-
specific skips.

Build fixtures and production-side probes only with MEMCOMP_KUNIT_TEST.
Include the split test headers from the production compilation unit and
provide a dedicated KUnit configuration. Controlled allocation, object-
cgroup and I/O models expose otherwise difficult lifetime paths; they do
not substitute for end-to-end runtime validation.

